\subsubsection*{1.2 Infinite Generators}

Because generators produce values on demand, they can describe infinite
sequences. A generator whose body is \texttt{while True: yield ...}
never raises \texttt{StopIteration}.

To use an infinite generator, consume it lazily: call \texttt{next()}
for a finite number of values, or wrap it in a finite combinator such as
\texttt{itertools.islice}. Calling \texttt{list()} on an infinite
generator runs forever and raises \texttt{MemoryError}.

Fibonacci numbers are the classic infinite generator: two state
variables \texttt{a, b} are advanced on each iteration with the
simultaneous assignment \texttt{a, b = b, a + b}.
